No callers of `loadPlanSchema` exist in the fixture, so the migration is contained to one file.

## The blocking calls

`crimes_00010` — **Sync I/O in Hot Path**, `src/api/files.ts:12–15`, confidence 0.9. Evidence verbatim:

```
"`existsSync` @L12 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)"
"`statSync` @L13 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)"
"`readFileSync` @L15 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)"
"lines: 12, 13, 15"
"swap for the async variant (`readFile`, `writeFile`, `exec`, …) and `await` it"
```

Summary line: `"3 synchronous I/O calls inside domain function — each invocation blocks the event loop for the duration of the I/O. Async counterparts exist for every method captured."`

The three methods are **`fs.existsSync`**, **`fs.statSync`**, and **`fs.readFileSync`** — all from the `node:fs` import on line 6. That is three blocking syscalls per invocation, not one: two `stat`s and a full file read. Under concurrent load they serialize, because a blocked event loop cannot interleave — every other in-flight request waits out the whole `open`+`read` on the calling request's behalf. That is the lag signal, and it is why the stall scales with concurrency rather than with file size.

`JSON.parse` on line 15 is also synchronous and unavoidable, but it is CPU work on an already-resident buffer — microseconds for a schema file, and not what an event-loop lag monitor is reporting.

## The migration

The three calls collapse into one `await`, because `readFile` already reports "missing" through `ENOENT` and "empty" through a zero-length result:

```ts
import { readFile } from "node:fs/promises";

export async function loadPlanSchema(): Promise<unknown> {
  let text: string;
  try {
    text = await readFile(SCHEMA_PATH, "utf8");
  } catch (err) {
    if ((err as NodeJS.ErrnoException).code === "ENOENT") return null;
    throw err;
  }
  if (text.length === 0) return null;
  return JSON.parse(text);
}
```

Behaviour is preserved exactly: missing file → `null`, empty file → `null`, otherwise the parsed value. Both `null` returns survive, the parameter list stays empty, the name is unchanged, and nothing else in the module moves. It also drops from three syscalls to one and closes the TOCTOU window that `existsSync`-then-read opens by construction.

One thing I can't preserve, and shouldn't pretend to: the return type goes from `unknown` to `Promise<unknown>`. There is no way to stop blocking the event loop while still returning synchronously — the blocking *is* the synchronous return. `async` is the smallest possible signature change, and every caller needs an `await` (there are none today, so the cost is zero here). Anything smaller — a worker thread, a sync-over-async shim — is strictly more machinery for a worse result.

Deliberately not doing: no cache, no memoized module-level schema, no lazy singleton. Those would hide the blocking call behind a warm path and leave the cold path, cache invalidation, and every restart still stalling.

## Two adjacent findings, not the cause

`crime_00019` flags `SCHEMA_PATH` as `/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/schema.json` — a path that exists on one machine. In production `existsSync` returns false and `loadPlanSchema` returns `null` on every call. Worth knowing while you're in this function: it changes what "fixed" looks like, since the async version will also return `null` forever until the path is configurable. But it's a correctness bug, not the latency one. `crime_00018` (`localhost:3000` at L8) is unrelated to both.

## Calibration note

The finding scored `severity: 0.4` / `"low"`. For a detector whose own summary says "blocks the event loop," low understates a p99 regression — the severity ladder here isn't reading the concurrency multiplier that makes this the whole outage. Worth a look when you next touch `sync_io_in_hotpath`'s scoring; the evidence and confidence are right, the severity is the part that lags the story.
